    "taxonId": 53197,
    "summary": "Perennial flowering plant native to Europe and Western Asia. Its leaves have distinctive curled edges above a large basal rosette, and it has a large yellow, forked taproot. Young leaves are eaten boiled in several changes of water or raw in moderation; mature plants are too bitter, while thoroughly dried seeds are also edible.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Young leaves are boiled in several water changes or eaten raw moderately; mature leaves become too bitter. Thoroughly dried seeds are also edible.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p8", "s1:p10"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p8", "s1:p10"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 136231,
    "summary": "Large coniferous tree endemic to Australia, naturally concentrated in southeast Queensland with two very small isolated populations in the northeast. It has a distinctive egg-shaped crown, very large cones and rigid, sharp-tipped leaves. Its edible nuts are eaten raw, cooked, ground into paste or bread, or traditionally creek-fermented by Indigenous Australians; shoots are also eaten.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "Indigenous Australians eat nuts raw, cooked, immature, as paste or bread, and traditionally creek-fermented; shoots are also eaten.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p2", "s1:p3", "s1:p4", "s1:p5", "s1:p30", "s1:p31"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p30", "s1:p31"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 154189,
    "summary": "Moss with a nearly cosmopolitan distribution across parts of the Americas, Eurasia and New Zealand. It forms clumps or lawns whose bright yellow-green leaves can glow against reddish-brown stems, and its cylindrical spore capsules are strongly ribbed.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
